          %title Contents
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://math.unice.fr/~carlos/"} website
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#selected_writings"} Selected writings
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_lab_entries"}
                  Related
                  = succeed "Lab" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#selected_writings Selected writings
          %ul
            %li
              %em> Systems of Hodge bundles and uniformization
              , Ph.D. Thesis, Harvard Univ., Cambridge, MA, 1987; J. Amer. Math. Soc. 1 (1988), no. 4, 867–918;
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=90e:58026"} MR90e:58026
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1990994"} doi
            %li
              %em> Some families of local systems over smooth projective varieties
              , Ann. Math., Sec. Ser.
              = succeed ":2" do
                %strong 138
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/2946614"} doi
          %p
            On a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+theorem"} de Rham theorem
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+stack"} algbraic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stacks"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-stacks
            via an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal shape modality
            realizing the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+stack"} de Rham stack
          %ul
            %li#SimpsonTeleman
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Teleman"} Constantin Teleman
                %em
                  deRham theorem for
                  = succeed "-stacks" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                (1997) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://math.berkeley.edu/~teleman/math/simpson.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SimpsonTeleman-deRham.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %em> Homotopy over the complex numbers and generalized de Rham cohomology
                , in: Moduli of Vector Bundles, edited by Masaki Maruyama, CRC Press 1996
            %li
              %p
                %em> Secondary Kodaira-Spencer classes and nonabelian Dolbeault cohomology
                , arXiv:
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/alg-geom/9712020"} alg-geom/9712020
          %p
            On Deligne’s
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda-connection"} lambda-connection
          %ul
            %li
              C. T. Simpson,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Hodge filtration on nonabelian cohomology
            %li
              Carlos T. Simpson,
              %em A weight two phenomenon for the moduli space of rank one local systems on open varieties
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.2800"} arXiv:0710.2800
            %li
              C. T. Simpson,
              %em
                Iterated destability modifications for vector bundles with connection, Contemp. Math.
                %strong 522
                (2010) 183–206
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the notion of lambda-connection
              %a{:href => "https://math.univ-cotedazur.fr/~carlos/slides/deligneconf/slideA.html"} slides
            %li
              C. T. Simpson,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Geometricity of the Hodge filtration on the ∞-stack of perfect complexes over
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi DR
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{DR}
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0510269"} math.AG/0510269
              %a{:href => "http://www.mathjournals.org/mmj/vol9-3-2009/cont9-3-2009.html"} journal
          %p
            More on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+Hodge+theory"} nonabelian Hodge theory
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+bundles"} Higgs bundles
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+systems"} local systems
              , Publ. Mathématiques de l’IHÉS
              %strong 75
              (1992), p. 5-95 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=PMIHES_1992__75__5_0"}> numdam:PMIHES_1992__75__5_0
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=94d:32027"} MR94d:32027
            %li
              %em> Algebraic aspects of higher nonabelian Hodge theory
              , arXiv:
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9902067"} math.AG/9902067
          %p
            An early conception of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"}
              presentable
              = succeed "-categorues" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-toposes"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-toposes
            (the term seems to originate here) via (what later were called)
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+toposes"} model toposes
          %ul
            %li#Simpson99
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
              ,
              %em
                A Giraud-type characterization of the simplicial categories associated to closed model categories as
                = succeed "-pretopoi" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/9903167"}> arXiv:math/9903167
              )
          %p
            On
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/secondary+characteristic+classes"} secondary characteristic classes
          %ul
            %li
              Jaya N. Iyer,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"} Carlos Simpson
              %em Regulators of canonical extensions are torsion: the smooth divisor case
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.0372"}> arXiv:0707.0372
              )
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Theory+of+Higher+Categories"} Homotopy Theory of Higher Categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00449826/document"} hal:00449826
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1001.4071"} http://arxiv.org/abs/1001.4071
              )
          %p
            Lectures on
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
          %ul
            %li
              Nicole Mestrano, Carlos Simpson,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Stacks
              %a{:href => "https://hal.science/hal-03347136/document"} pdf
          %p
            On
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
          %p In January 2009 Carlos Simpson wrote the following short note
          %ul
            %li
              C. Simpson,
              %em Descent
              (
              %a{:href => "http://math.unice.fr/~carlos/slides/ihesAGjan09.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            The following reproduces the text unabridged, but equipped with hyperlinks on keywords to
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            “ The notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            = succeed "es\u201C" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            = succeed "s\u201C" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chart"} chart
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} Descent
            comes into Grothendieck’s philosophy and work in a myriad of forms, starting with his papers
            %em Technique de descente et théorèmes
          %p
            d’existence en géométrie algébrique_ . The technical requirements of this theory incited him to introduce the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+category"} fibered category
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
            and giving us
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+cohomology"} etale cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            used in Deligne’s papers on Hodge theory, where
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial object
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
          %p
            Between the 1960’s and the 1980’s, the notion of descent for objects of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+space"} Eilenberg-MacLane
            presheaves, and the theory of twisted complexes of Toledo and Tong.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"} Stasheff
            and Wirth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} investigated higher cocycles
            in the 1960’s although Wirth’s thesis was only recently
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/09/wirth_and_stasheff_on_homotopy.html"} made public
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model categories
            was gradually applied to simplicial diagrams. Illusie introduced the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} weak equivalence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+presheaf"} simplicial presheaves
            on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} Grothendieck site
          %p
            Grothendieck came out of isolation with the manuscript
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} La poursuite des champs
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sheaves"} model category structure on simplicial sheaves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model category structure for simplicial presheaves
            enhancing Illusie’s weak equivalences. We enter into the modern period in which Jardine’s model structure and its variants have been used and developped with applications in a wide range of mathematics including Thomason’s work in K-theory and then Voevodsky’s theory of
            = succeed "-homotopy" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^1
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motive"} motive
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+stack"} Algebraic stack
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
          %p
            In Grothendieck’s vision as set out in “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"}> La poursuite des champs
            ”, higher descent is just the same as usual descent, but for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"} n-stacks
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-categories
            over a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-categories
            was developped early on by Benabou, having occured also in the book of Gabriel and Zisman. The theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-category"} strict n-categories
            was thoroughly investigated by Street and the Australian school, and Brown and Loday introduced other related algebraic objects which could model
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/omega-category"} weak n-categories
            where composition would be associative only up to a coherent system of higher equivalences. Similar ideas were being developped by Dwyer and Kan in algebraic topology, and Cordier and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"} Porter
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+omega-category"} weak n-categories
            have been proposed, by Baez-Dolan, Tamsamani, Batanin and others. It is now well understood that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coherent+category+theory"} homotopy coherence
            problems inherent in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topologists
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping+hypothesis"} delooping machines
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} La poursuite des champs
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            My own work in this area is inspired by the phrase in “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"}> La poursuite des champs
            ” where Grothendieck forsees
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"} n-stacks
            as the natural coefficients for higher
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"} n-stack
            based on Tamsamani’s definition of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mi stacks
                  %mi on
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto \{n-stacks on U\}
            is an
            = succeed "-stack." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
          %p
            The theory of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"}> derived algebraic geometry
            ” originated by Kontsevich, Kapranov and Ciocan-Fontanine is now cast by Toen, Vezzosi and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Lurie
            in a foundational framework which relies on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"} higher stacks
            for glueing. In the future derived geometry should be a key ingredient in Hodge theory for higher
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katzarkov"} Katzarkov
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Toen"} Toen
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schematic+homotopy+type"} schematic homotopy type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} La poursuite des champs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Treumann"} Treumann
            and Dupont go in this direction by using exit-path n-categories to classify constructible complexes of sheaves.
          %p
            More recent developments include
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/09/wirth_and_stasheff_on_homotopy.html"} Hopkins and Lurie’s proof
            of a part of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+tangle+hypothesis"} Baez-Dolan system of conjectures
            relating higher categories to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} topological
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            permits us to imagine a local notion of descent as was explained to me by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ben-Zvi"} David Ben-Zvi
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"} Stasheff
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            into a local formal space, with the neighborhood intersection being the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+infinity-function+theory"} derived loop space of Ben-Zvi and Nadler
          %h2#related_lab_entries
